<p>Prime Minister Narendra Modi’s Bharatiya Janata Party defeated Mamata Banerjee’s Trinamool Congress in West Bengal’s state election, ending the TMC’s rule that began in 2011 and giving the BJP its first government in the eastern state bordering Bangladesh. The BJP secured more than two-thirds of the 294-seat assembly, according to vote counts reported Monday, while Banerjee’s party fell sharply from its previous majority and she lost her own seat. Banerjee said Tuesday that she would not resign, alleged that about 100 seats were “forcibly taken” from her party and accused the Election Commission of bias. She did not provide evidence, and West Bengal Chief Electoral Officer Manoj Kumar Agarwal called the allegations “baseless”. The dispute left the timing of the handover uncertain.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/dpEm3MnLHE-SHpl8ZshKCJt-ZOr8oHU-5yYuLTcs-HZPN_xy9Fhq_xYLeq7bj7DIjV2YRiDBB4GGmOwdrWZPTv4Rels1brDf70Nr4h6dYQ3H' alt='Chief Minister of West Bengal and Chairperson of All India Trinamool Congress Mamata Banerjee addresses a press conference at her residence office in Kolkata on May 5, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Seat counts: Reported tallies varied slightly by source, with reports carrying AFP and Dhaka Tribune citing 206 BJP seats, Scroll reporting 207 and NDTV reporting 208.</li><li>Adhikari background: Adhikari was formerly in Banerjee’s camp, crossed over to the BJP and served as Leader of Opposition in the West Bengal Assembly.</li><li>Chief minister: The BJP had not announced its West Bengal chief minister as of Wednesday, with NDTV and The Week reporting that Adhikari was among the names under discussion.</li><li>Other contests: In the same round of elections, the BJP retained Assam and Puducherry, while Kerala and Tamil Nadu also saw changes of government.</li></ul><blockquote>I will not resign, I did not lose... officially, through the Election Commission, they (the BJP) can defeat us, but morally, we won the election - Mamata 